# K3 - Install channels and strict/permissive contracts. Kata sandbox sketch (plan section 3.6).
# [K3-nn] sketch IDs are recognised by validate_architecture / trace and ignored by the
# CI adl-coverage gate. Never [TR-*]; promotion is decided only at K12 (architecture/REVIEW.md).
DESCRIPTION K3 - which shapes cross a release boundary, and which names old installers depend on
CATEGORY Static Coupling
NAMESPACE TrK3NS
DEFINE SYSTEM Total Recall K3 Sandbox AS trk3
    DEFINE COMPONENT Contract Coverage AS coverage
    DEFINE COMPONENT Installer Names AS installer

# Course Task 2: classify each contract strict or permissive, by who controls the deployment of
# both ends. contracts.adl does this for tool calls, hook JSON and memory files. The org index.json
# is the fourth shape that crosses a version boundary (one client writes, a teammate on another
# version reads, through git), and it has a version guard in code (ORG_INDEX_VERSION: refuse to
# write over a newer file, read the legacy flat shape) but no contract in the ADL.
ASSERT(Contract Coverage: every shape the code shows crossing a release boundary has a DEFINE CONTRACT in contracts.adl) # [K3-01]

# Course Task 1 and 3 (the tablet app in the field): an old npx bootstrap stays on npm and in npx
# caches. It adds the marketplace by name and installs total-recall@<name> from the release ref, so
# those names and that ref can never change without stranding old installers.
ASSERT(Installer Names: the bootstrap's marketplace name, the plugin name and the release ref are the ones marketplace.json declares) # [K3-02]

# Not re-asserted, answered with existing evidence (answers.md): hooks.json vs hooks.gemini.json
# parity (hooks-parity.test.ts), tool-schema additivity (TR-C1), tolerant memory files (TR-C6),
# version skew between channels (TR-Q3).
